{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nRebar's plan-review T10 and code-review IaC agents can inspect Terraform as text, but generic file search does not reliably answer module-wide questions: Terraform loads every `.tf` and `.tf.json` file in a module together, local modules cross directory boundaries, and computed values are unknowable without runtime context. That makes unsupported LLM claims about declarations, references, and sources a false-positive/false-negative risk.\n\nThis feature supplies query-scoped evidence for LLM findings. It does not duplicate mechanical Terraform validation that belongs in project CI, and it never executes Terraform, OpenTofu, a provider binary, or an apply/plan/validate operation.\n\n## Outcome\n\nShip an optional `grounding-terraform` extra containing `python-hcl2==8.1.3`. When installed, Terraform tools are exposed only to Terraform-routed Pass-1 and Pass-2 agents in plan review and code review. Core Rebar and projects without Terraform retain the same dependency closure and tool surface.\n\nThe final tool surface is:\n\n- `lookup_declaration`: locate a statically addressable declaration and its source location.\n- `resolve_reference`: resolve conservative module-scope traversals, including local child outputs, without evaluating expressions or provider schemas.\n- `probe_source`: inspect repo-contained local sources and positive registry metadata reachable with existing ambient credentials; it never downloads module contents.\n- `corroborate_diagnostic`: independently match a narrow structural diagnostic using an audited `terraform-config-inspect` binary already on `PATH`.\n\nEvery call returns the existing `refuted | match | abstain` evidence shape plus a canonical receipt containing the normalized query, code/module digest, backend/version/config, outcome, and result digest. Tool output contains source paths, spans, structural classifications, and hashes, but never credentials or raw literal values.\n\n## Scope and mechanics\n\n- Scope starts from changed or declared `.tf` / `.tf.json` paths. Each affected directory is indexed as one whole Terraform module. The bounded closure follows literal, repo-contained local module sources forward and discovers in-repo literal reverse callers. A changed `.tfvars` file is inspectable directly but is never treated as selected input: Rebar cannot know which variable file Terraform would load.\n- A per-agent-call session owns an immutable parse cache and query ledger. Pass 2 must issue its own tool query; it may reuse immutable parse data but cannot accept a Pass-1 receipt as verification.\n- The parser receives already-read file bytes in the existing 60-second grounding worker boundary. Out-of-repo paths and escaping symlinks are rejected. Syntax errors, duplicate/ambiguous declarations, computed values, provider-defined attributes, dynamic sources, and runtime-dependent expressions abstain.\n- The whole query abstains without partial conclusions if its closure exceeds 64 modules, 5,000 Terraform files, or 32 MiB.\n- Actual repository reads are merged into agent usage as synthetic file reads. Membership globs for `**/*.tf` and `**/*.tf.json` use the existing signed read-set glob hashing so a later sibling file addition invalidates plan-review evidence without requiring the optional parser at claim time.\n- Registry access is positive-only. Environment `TF_TOKEN_<hostname>` credentials and static Terraform credential files may be used with Terraform's precedence rules. Rebar never prompts, executes credential helpers, returns tokens, or treats 401/403/404, TLS/proxy failures, timeouts, rate limits, or malformed responses as proof that a source does not exist.\n- V1 does not fetch remote module contents. The ADR records that remote fetch using ambient credentials is approved as a future extension if demand justifies it, with the same containment, redaction, and abstention rules.\n- `terraform-config-inspect` is the only optional executable allowed in V1. It receives a minimal copied snapshot of approved module files, closed stdin, a scrubbed environment, and a bounded process group; its JSON is schema-checked and its absolute path, binary digest, and discoverable version are recorded. Absence, output skew, errors, or a non-match abstain. TFLint, Trivy, tfparse, terraform-ls, terraform-docs, Terraform, OpenTofu, and provider executables are not invoked.\n\n## Positive rationale\n\nConservative structural evidence addresses the LLM's uncertainty while keeping deterministic validation in client CI. `python-hcl2` owns HCL parsing, HashiCorp's shallow `terraform-config-inspect` provides optional independent corroboration, and Rebar owns only safe orchestration, scope closure, redaction, receipts, and review routing. Abstention is preferable to recreating Terraform evaluation semantics.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Running `terraform validate`, `terraform plan`, `terraform init/get`, OpenTofu, providers, policy scanners, plugins, or repository-supplied executable/config hooks.\n- Evaluating expressions, cloud state, provider schemas, variable precedence, or whether an arbitrary `.tfvars` file is selected.\n- Accepting a caller-produced plan artifact; the subject is Terraform in the client project running Rebar.\n- Downloading or executing remote module content in V1.\n- Turning grounding into a scanner or a standalone CI validator. Tools answer agent queries and do not originate findings.\n\n## Decomposition\n\n1. A plan-review vertical slice ships the optional parser/index, `lookup_declaration`, `resolve_reference`, signed read receipts, and Terraform-only Pass-1/Pass-2 routing.\n2. A dependent code-review slice reuses that session for the IaC overlay and its verifier.\n3. A dependent source-probe slice adds local and positive registry-source evidence with ambient-credential redaction and access-error abstention.\n4. A dependent authoritative-corroboration slice adds the sole allowlisted `terraform-config-inspect` adapter and live contract canary.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Installing `nava-rebar[grounding-terraform]` adds exactly `python-hcl2==8.1.3`; lean-core subprocess tests prove `import rebar` and non-Terraform reviews neither import `hcl2` nor advertise Terraform tools.\n- [ ] Plan-review T10 Pass 1 and evidence-bearing Pass 2 can independently call `lookup_declaration` and `resolve_reference` over the complete bounded affected-module closure; non-Terraform criteria cannot see those tools.\n- [ ] Code-review IaC Pass 1 and evidence-bearing Pass 2 receive the same grounding operations for changed Terraform, while non-IaC overlays and reviews do not.\n- [ ] `probe_source` returns positive local/registry evidence only from repo-contained files or metadata reachable with ambient credentials, and every access/error/ambiguity arm returns a closed abstention without asserting nonexistence.\n- [ ] `corroborate_diagnostic` can report only a matching, schema-valid `terraform-config-inspect` result from the hardened snapshot; a clean/absent/skewed/error result abstains and no other Terraform-related executable is launched.\n- [ ] Canonical receipts redact credentials and literal payloads, and signed plan-review read sets include every concrete Terraform file read plus membership protection that detects a newly added module sibling.\n- [ ] `docs/adr/0111-terraform-structural-grounding.md` records the trust boundary, OSS rationale, V1 prohibitions, and approved future ambient-credential remote-fetch direction; `docs/grounding.md` documents install, routing, limits, outcomes, and abstentions.\n\n## Verification\n\nEach child carries tier-specific commands and observable oracles. The epic is verified by the union of those child criteria; no live Terraform/cloud execution is part of completion.","title":"Optional Terraform structural grounding for IaC review agents"}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788469313499205231,"uuid":"cd1c32f7-2da7-413a-b70e-fd02b68b9fb8"}